WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:02.600
This episode is sponsored by Audible.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:11.600
To get started, go to audible.com/microcosmos
or text “microcosmos” to 500 500.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:39.200
The microcosmos surrounds and engulfs, invisible
to us until we peer through the lens of our

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:47.579
microscope to witness the incredible diversity
of life at what seems like the smallest of scales.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:56.760
But there are much, much smaller scales than
this, creating a kind of sub-microcosmos.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.600
And while you may find some exceptionally
tiny life here, this is also the realm of

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:10.780
viruses, existing in their own limbo between
what we consider living and non-living.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:16.830
Viruses are built of packaged genetic material,
just like any living cell.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:24.420
But unlike microbes and our own cells, viruses
lack the ability to replicate that genetic material.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:31.960
So instead, they infect, relying on their
hosts to take in their genetic material and

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:37.130
follow their instructions to make more virus.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:43.320
While there are some large viruses, like the
amoeba-infecting megaviruses that can get

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:49.090
as large as 750 nanometers, most are much,
much smaller.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:55.420
For context, the little crystals you see illuminated
at the tip of this closterium are about 2

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.170
microns, or 2000 nanometers long.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:07.980
That's almost 3 times the size of the largest
megavirus, and almost 17 times the size of

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.850
most coronaviruses.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:16.540
So it is difficult to see viruses with the
equipment we have on hand.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:23.519
But this sub-microcosmos has a long reach,
extending into the microcosmos and as well

00:02:23.519 --> 00:02:25.940
into our own world.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:32.240
So for today, we want to talk more about some
of the ties that bind across scale.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:45.319
Let's start with one example of how viruses
shape life in the microcosmos.

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:49.920
These paramecium bursaria are very, very green.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.310
They get that color thanks to hundreds of
little green algae called Chlorella that are

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.650
housed in vacuoles.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:05.799
This relationship between the paramecium and
its internal algae is an example of endosymbiosis.

00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:11.030
And when we see examples of endosymbiosis
in the microcosmos, it's easy to focus on

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:14.670
what the larger microbe is getting out of
the relationship.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:20.540
The green algae are photosynthetic, which
makes them a great internal nutrition provider

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.249
to the paramecium.

00:03:22.249 --> 00:03:26.430
But symbiosis is a complicated relationship
to maintain.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:32.760
The Chlorella have to be housed in a special
perialgal vacuole membrane.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:38.090
And the paramecium can control the number
of chlorella based on how much light there

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.180
is or based on when the paramecium is getting
ready to divide.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:48.090
So why would the algae agree to all of this?

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.430
Well, protection.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:58.380
The microcosmos, as we’ve seen, is full
of predators both small and a little less small.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:05.180
But just as worrisome to the Chlorella are
the viruses, particularly the Phycodnaviridae,

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:11.389
a group of large, DNA viruses that target
eukaryotic algae.

00:04:11.389 --> 00:04:16.390
From this group, Chlorella are particularly
susceptible to two types of viruses called

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:20.480
NC64A and Pbi.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.120
Look, once you get down to these scales, nobody’s
thinking of creative names anymore.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.300
But when they're inside the paramecium, the
Chlorella are safe.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:36.240
It's like the paramecium is a kind of fence,
keeping the algae in and the viruses out.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.650
These viruses only target the chlorella when
they are released from the safe confines of

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.310
the paramecium.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:48.680
Of course, viruses are also crafty.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:54.840
Scientists studying chlorella viruses found
that they were able to wait along the surface

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:03.160
of paramecium bursaria, most likely binding
to receptors that keep them anchored to the microbe.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:10.120
These videos were produced by the researchers
using confocal microscopy and fluorescent stains.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:15.510
The big red blobs you see are actually the
Chlorella’s chloroplasts, distributed around

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:20.220
the paramecium and making their own red autofluorescence.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:26.590
The green dots are produced by adding a stain
that binds to the DNA of dead cells, showing

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:31.430
us potential locations for the virus along
the outside of the cell.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:38.340
If the paramecium dies for whatever reason,
the Chlorella inside of them get released.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:45.250
And now, not only is the Chlorella's protector
gone, its membrane is lined with the very

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:49.780
thing the algae was trying to evade.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:50.680
Terrifying.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:00.980
The goal for viruses is to infect, to find
something that will make more of them.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.540
Sometimes they're looking to infect an algae,
sometimes they're looking to infect one of

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:06.740
our own cells.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:13.290
And in a way, you can think of our skin the
same way chlorella might see a paramecium.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:19.150
It’s the initial line of defense against
pathogens like viruses, protecting the many

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:21.500
cells inside us.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.430
There are other ways that viruses infect us,
but in addition to our own body's defenses,

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:33.070
humans have created a very simple but powerful
tool for dealing with pathogens--one that

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:39.120
we didn't even realize was so powerful until
a few centuries ago: soap.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.560
Beautiful, beautiful soap.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:50.470
Soap has been around for many, many millennia,
made from various oils and alkali.

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:55.280
The goal was cleanliness: whether of objects
or of a person's own body.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:01.040
But it wasn't until the mid-1800s that a few
doctors began to realize that washing their

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:05.930
hands kept their patients safer and healthier.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:10.190
But even as we began to understand more about
microbes and people continued to push for

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:18.050
soap as a way to contend with pathogens, it
wasn't until the 1980s that anyone established

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:22.690
some sort of national hand-washing guideline
for hospitals.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:27.370
So what is so magic about soap and washing
your hands?

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:33.290
Well, some of it is just the movement of it
all, of water and friction scrubbing away

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:36.710
at things that you might not even be able
to see.

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:41.470
But part of the power of soap is simple chemistry.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:45.820
We can't really demonstrate the effect of
soap on viruses because, as we said earlier,

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.030
we don't really have the equipment to showcase
them.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:53.850
So we're going to use bacteria as a proxy
instead for a simple experiment.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:59.470
While bacteria and viruses are different in
a number of ways, they also share one very

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.820
important trait.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:07.530
These bacteria were obtained most professionally
from the saliva of some very cute kittens

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.640
named Lupin and Sirius.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.780
That’s right, we swabbed our kitties.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:18.050
The membranes of these bacteria are made up
of lipids, which have a phosphate head which

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:22.860
dissolves in water and two fatty acid chain
tails that don't.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.710
The structure of this membrane is built on
keeping these fatty acid chains away from

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:34.249
the water that is both inside and outside
the bacteria, and that creates what's called

00:08:34.249 --> 00:08:36.149
the lipid bilayer

00:08:36.149 --> 00:08:43.719
And while viruses aren't technically living,
they can find themselves reliant on a similar chemistry.

00:08:43.719 --> 00:08:48.079
Their genetic material is enclosed in a protein
capsid.

00:08:48.079 --> 00:08:53.480
And then for many viruses, that capsid is
coated with a viral envelope that, like the

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:58.319
bacterial membrane, is made up of a lipid
bilayer.

00:08:58.319 --> 00:09:05.139
Soap has a structure similar to these lipids,
with a fatty acid tail that wants to avoid water.

00:09:05.139 --> 00:09:12.399
And as we pipette diluted liquid soap onto
the sample, those hydrophobic tails also want

00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:13.980
to avoid water.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:21.970
Some do this by attempting to break into the
bacteria's lipid bilayer, ultimately breaking

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:25.029
up the membrane entirely.

00:09:25.029 --> 00:09:30.329
Others form bubbles called micelles around
bits of bacteria and other debris.

00:09:30.329 --> 00:09:36.760
Of course, we can't see these little chemical
disruptions in action, that’s far too small a scale.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:43.020
What we do see is the bacterial colony quickly
melting away.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.960
Our own cells also have lipid bilayers.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:10:00.740
But we can wash our hands without them completely
dissolving away into nothing because our skin

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:05.839
is a complex combination of protein and cells
that protects us.

00:10:05.839 --> 00:10:12.079
Instead, the soapy water destroys and carries
away the microbes that cover them, making

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:17.780
it safer to eat and to touch and whatever
else you might be using your hands for.

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:19.560
Viruses are tricky.

00:10:19.569 --> 00:10:23.459
They're so small and so capable of adaptation.

00:10:23.459 --> 00:10:29.129
So sometimes you have to hide out like an
algae, and other times you can rely on the

00:10:29.129 --> 00:10:31.600
basic chemistry of soap.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:37.470
But these are only two examples of the many,
many strategies that have evolved in nature

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:43.730
or been developed by humans to contend with
the enormous impact that viruses can have

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:44.920
on our lives.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:51.459
Nothing in this world is so small that we
cannot find a way to understand it, and nothing

00:10:51.459 --> 00:10:56.449
is so large that we cannot seek to confront
it.

00:10:56.449 --> 00:11:02.339
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

